snippet.textDisplay:
@heels4lifx no one chooses to have an addiction. If you want accountability, you can say "it's their fault" or "you did this to yourself". They chose to drink, they didn't choose to be alcoholic. Because a choice is not the result of the decision, it's the decision itself. There are always avenues to get out, to choose to be in a bad position is to blatantly ignore them. Struggling to get to that avenue is not choosing to be in a bad position.
To say someone "chooses" only serves to generate ragebait, views, and misunderstandings. There's no reason to do that.

Regardless, most people don't even need to be held accountable because they already know what they did wrong. They need a way out. Being accountable is important but that shouldn't be the focus, yet, it is.

